> This is a classic "lan dan" (lazy egg) technique—the chicken cooks in the hot liquid off the boil, resulting in impossibly tender and juicy meat. Don't rush the rest at the end; it's what makes the difference between good and restaurant-quality.

== Prepare the Braising Liquid ==

In a #large pot{}, combine:
- @light soy sauce{250%ml}
- @dark soy sauce{140%ml}
- @sugar{100%g}
- @Shaoxing wine{60%ml}
- @water{700%ml}
- @ginger{4%slices}
- @scallions{4%stalks} (cut into 5 cm pieces)
- @star anise{3%whole}
- @cinnamon stick{1}

Bring to a boil over medium-high heat, stirring to dissolve the sugar.

== Braise the Chicken ==

Carefully lower the @bone-in, skin-on chicken thighs{1.2%kg} into the liquid skin-side up. The liquid should barely cover the chicken.

Reduce the heat to a gentle simmer—the liquid should be trembling with tiny bubbles, not rolling. Cover and cook for ~{30%minutes}.

== Rest and Serve ==

Turn off the heat. Leave the pot covered and let the chicken rest in the hot liquid for another ~{15%minutes}. This residual heat finishes cooking the chicken while the soy glaze penetrates the meat.

Remove the chicken and chop into serving pieces. Drizzle with a spoonful of the braising liquid for shine and flavor.

Strain the remaining braising liquid and save it—it makes an excellent master stock for noodles or dumplings, or simmer hard-boiled eggs in it overnight for a breakfast treat.